---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import Code from '../../../components/docs/Code.astro';
import DeviceConstraints from '../../../components/docs/DeviceConstraints.astro';
import { page } from '../../../data/docs-nav';

const entry = page('/docs/destinations/mqtt/');

const topics = `hozz/batch          the whole batch, in the destination's format
hozz/heart_rate     the latest point of that metric
hozz/step_count     the latest point of that metric
hozz/sleep_analysis the latest point of that metric`;

const point = `{
  "date": "2026-08-22T21:10:46.500Z",
  "qty": 62.5,
  "source": "Apple Watch",
  "units": "count/min"
}`;

const haSensor = `mqtt:
  sensor:
    - name: "Heart rate"
      state_topic: "hozz/heart_rate"
      value_template: "{{ value_json.qty }}"
      unit_of_measurement: "bpm"
      device_class: measurement`;
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Destinations">
  <div class="fact-strip">
    <div>
      <dt>Format</dt>
      <dd>Metrics JSON</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Protocol</dt>
      <dd>MQTT 3.1.1, QoS 0, retained</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Schemes</dt>
      <dd><code>mqtt://</code> and <code>mqtts://</code></dd>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Hozz publishes to an MQTT broker on your network. If a broker is already the centre of your
    home setup, this puts Health data on it alongside everything else, and anything already
    subscribing can pick it up without knowing Hozz exists.
  </p>

  <h2>Setting it up</h2>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Enter the broker address</h3>
      <p>
        For example <code>mqtt://homeassistant.local:1883</code>. Use <code>mqtts://</code> for TLS.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Add a username and password if your broker requires them</h3>
      <p>Credentials go to the device Keychain, not into the destination record.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Send a test</h3>
      <p>
        Subscribe to <code>hozz/#</code> in any MQTT client and you will see the probe arrive. It
        carries no Health data.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Topics</h2>

  <p>
    The whole batch goes to <code>&lt;root&gt;/batch</code>. For Metrics JSON only, the{' '}
    <strong>latest point of each metric</strong> also goes to <code>&lt;root&gt;/&lt;metric&gt;</code>.{' '}
    <code>&lt;root&gt;</code> defaults to <code>hozz</code>.
  </p>

  <Code caption="Topics published for a Metrics JSON batch" code={topics} />

  <p>
    The per-metric topics are the point of using MQTT here: a sensor can subscribe to{' '}
    <code>hozz/step_count</code> and read a value, rather than subscribing to a batch and parsing it.
    Other formats publish only <code>&lt;root&gt;/batch</code>, because there is nothing to split per
    metric.
  </p>

  <Code caption="Payload on hozz/heart_rate" code={point} />

  <p>
    Metric names are the same lowercase snake_case names used everywhere else —{' '}
    <a href="/docs/delivery-schema/#metric-names">see the schema</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Retained messages</h2>

  <p>
    Per-metric topics are published <strong>retained, at QoS 0</strong>, so a subscriber that
    connects later immediately receives the last known value rather than waiting for the next
    delivery. That is what makes a dashboard show something the moment it starts.
  </p>

  <h2>Using it from Home Assistant</h2>

  <Code caption="configuration.yaml" code={haSensor} />

  <p>
    If you would rather not run a broker at all, <a href="/docs/destinations/home-assistant/">the
      Home Assistant destination</a> posts the same data straight to a webhook.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="MQTT keeps no history">
    <p>
      This is the honest limitation, and the app says so where you choose it. A broker that is
      offline misses that batch. Hozz retries, but a{' '}
      <a href="/docs/destinations/folder/">folder</a> is more forgiving — it keeps every batch as a
      file, and a computer that was switched off simply picks them up later.
    </p>
    <p>
      Retained per-metric topics also only keep the <em>latest</em> point, by design. They are a
      current-value surface, not a record. If you want every sample, subscribe to{' '}
      <code>&lt;root&gt;/batch</code> or send NDJSON somewhere alongside.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>What Hozz implements</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz speaks the part of MQTT 3.1.1 a publisher needs: CONNECT, PUBLISH at QoS 0, and DISCONNECT.
    It is not a full client, and it does not subscribe to anything. If your broker requires
    features beyond that, this destination will not be the right one.
  </p>

  <DeviceConstraints />
</DocsLayout>
